00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:24.917
Tell us about QDox.

00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:25.750
HARSHIT MALIK: Sure.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:30.070
So QDox is an enterprise grade
productized document processing

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:31.090
solution.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.820
So it's designed to handle
oppressive workloads

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:37.090
of converting documents
into usable data.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:41.140
So it uses a combination of
traditional OCR and custom

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.680
deep learning models for
handling virtually any template

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:47.950
of documents and to convert
into a knowledge base which

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.640
can be leveraged for any
further business uses.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.640
We have a ton of features
available in QDox,

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:59.320
so for example, entity
recognition, key value bearing,

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.200
digitizing unstructured
and structured tables,

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.920
digitizing charts,
graphs, maps, and so on.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.520
And also under the hood, we
have an active learning pipeline

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.350
which ensures continuous
improvement of our ML models.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:14.440
So any feedback
the user provides,

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.870
helps us in improving our
own ML models, as well.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:18.370
LORIN PRICE: Can
you talk about some

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.830
of the specific
technologies that were used

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:22.210
when you were building QDox?

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:23.293
HARSHIT MALIK: Absolutely.

00:02:23.293 --> 00:02:25.630
In order to keep our
design as scalable

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:29.620
and customizable as possible,
we used a lot of GCP tools

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:30.820
for building a solution.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:33.370
From the start to the
end, basically, the user

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:35.920
has the option of
uploading their documents

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.130
to Google Cloud via multiple
sources, like you UI

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:42.220
or emails or simple
drag and drop.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.950
All of these documents are
stored in Google Cloud Storage

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:48.790
as a staging area, which
triggers a Cloud Task which

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:52.630
sends out asynchronous triggers
to App Engine for processing

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:53.830
these documents.

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:58.120
So we use Vision API for
converting this image

00:02:58.120 --> 00:02:59.920
and document into text.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.000
And we also have
custom ML models,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.000
which are containerized
and stored

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.370
in App Engine for handling
all of the ML tasks.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:09.990
Now, the results can be stored
in various data storages.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.350
So if you want
relational database,

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:14.770
you can use, for example,
a BigQuery or Cloud SQL,

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:16.240
or if you want a
no SQL database,

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:19.000
you can use Bigtable
or Firestore.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.600
And if you also want any
visualization or business

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:25.420
analytics, you can
port these results

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:28.140
to any analytics
tool of your choice,

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.247
say Looker or Tableau
or Data Studio.

00:03:30.247 --> 00:03:32.080
LORIN PRICE: What are
some of the challenges

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:34.310
that you encountered
when building QDox?

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:37.390
HARSHIT MALIK: We designed
QDox with an evolutionary

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:40.810
architecture so that it's
capable of recognizing

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:44.540
any challenges and adapting
to it in the future.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:48.280
So some of the challenges
we faced were firstly,

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:51.350
we had data shortage issue.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.740
So we realized that
if we need to provide

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:56.320
high standards of
accuracy, we might not

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.220
be able to cover all templates.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:01.760
So user feedback is very
important in that scenario.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.190
So we included the
active learning pipeline.

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:06.790
So anytime a user
provides feedback

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:09.430
for a particular document,
that document becomes a data

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:11.890
point which helps in
retraining our models

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:15.280
and ensuring that the accuracy
level keeps on increasing.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.140
Secondly, another
challenge which we faced

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.990
was that earlier, we
had used Cloud Functions

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:21.910
for deploying our ML models.

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:24.640
What we found out that our ML
models were too computationally

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.985
heavy, and we would often
face a timeout issue

00:04:26.985 --> 00:04:28.510
in Cloud Functions.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:32.050
So instead, we used App
Engine, which integrates well

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:36.100
with Cloud Tasks, and it's
also able to scale up endlessly

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.348
for our ML models, as well.

00:04:37.348 --> 00:04:38.140
LORIN PRICE: Great.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:40.990
Well, Harshit, thank you so much
for being here with us today

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:42.545
and for sharing more about QDox.

00:04:42.545 --> 00:04:44.170
HARSHIT MALIK: Thank
you for having us.

